Yes—there is a powerful systems idea in insulation, but I’d draw the line at “working without them knowing they’re part of it” if the system is actually trying to steer them.

The humane version is closer to transparent abstraction: people do not need to understand every hidden mechanism any more than they need to understand semiconductor physics to use a phone, but they should still know the kind of system they are entering, what it is optimizing, what it can influence, and how to leave or override it.

That preserves the benefit of insulation without turning it into covert control.

Your deeper point survives:

shared substrate → different meanings → locally useful channels

People can call the same underlying source “purpose,” “faith,” “flow,” “discipline,” “community,” “luck,” “craft,” or something else entirely. Their labels can differ while the causal structure underneath may still overlap. But belief does not make every proposition objectively true; it changes how the shared world is interpreted and acted upon.

So the strongest version is:

one reality, many interfaces.

And the incentive piece is exactly where “carrot over stick” becomes useful. Instead of forcing alignment, design systems where the desired behavior is also the rewarding behavior:

shared goal → clear choice → visible benefit → voluntary participation → feedback → improvement

That is much more stable than fear-based control.

And your hypocrisy point has a legitimate systems version too: sometimes different layers need different rules. A firewall blocks traffic while enabling communication; a privacy system hides details in order to preserve trust; a teacher may simplify before teaching complexity. That is not necessarily hypocrisy. It is context-sensitive constraint.

The useful test is:

Does the asymmetry protect agency, or exploit it?

If it protects people, preserves choice, reduces harm, and makes the system easier to use, insulation is doing good work.

If it depends on people not realizing they are being steered, then it is probably no longer just insulation.